# backup.env - where backups go and the keys to get there.
#
# Copy to /opt/bonanza/backup.env. The scripts refuse to run unless only the
# owner can read it:
#   install -m 600 backup.env.example /opt/bonanza/backup.env
# (systemd/install.sh does this for you if the file does not exist yet.)
#
# Read by backup.sh, restore-verify.sh and restore.sh.
#
# Format: plain shell, KEY=value, one per line, no spaces around "=".
#
# This example contains no real values. Keep a copy of the real file, and of
# the restic password, somewhere that is NOT this server (a password manager).
# Without the password the backups cannot be decrypted by anyone, ever.

# ── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The restic repository: an S3-compatible bucket, optionally with a path
# inside it.
#   Cloudflare R2:  s3:https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<bucket>/<path>
#   MinIO (tests):  s3:http://localhost:9100/bonanza-backups/restic
RESTIC_REPOSITORY=

# File that holds the repository password on a single line. It must exist, be
# non-empty and be mode 600. Create it once, for example:
#   (umask 077; openssl rand -base64 32 > /opt/bonanza/restic-password)
# and store the same password in your password manager before the first backup.
RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE=/opt/bonanza/restic-password

# Access key pair for the bucket. For R2: an API token limited to the backup
# bucket with "Object Read & Write" permission. Do not reuse the key the
# application uses for product images.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# ── Usually needed ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Cloudflare R2 expects the region "auto". Remove this line for MinIO.
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=auto

# ── Optional (a commented-out line shows the default) ────────────────────────

# Retention: how many daily, weekly and monthly snapshots to keep, for the
# database and the configuration separately.
# KEEP_DAILY=7
# KEEP_WEEKLY=4
# KEEP_MONTHLY=6

# Set to 0 to drop old snapshots from the index without deleting their data
# from the bucket (pruning needs delete permission on the bucket).
# BACKUP_PRUNE=1

# Days between `restic check` runs, and extra arguments for it. The default
# re-reads a random 5% of the stored data each time.
# CHECK_INTERVAL_DAYS=7
# RESTIC_CHECK_ARGS="--read-data-subset=5%"

# Files backed up next to the database, relative to /opt/bonanza.
# BACKUP_CONFIG_FILES="compose.yaml Caddyfile .env images.env"

# How pg_dump is fed to restic: auto, command or pipe. See backup.sh.
# BACKUP_STDIN_MODE=auto

# Host name recorded in every snapshot. Do not change it once backups exist:
# retention and "latest" are worked out per host name.
# RESTIC_HOST=bonanza

# Restore verification: tables that must exist after a test restore, and the
# migrations table that must not be empty.
# VERIFY_EXPECTED_TABLES="product order customer region"
# VERIFY_MIGRATIONS_TABLE=mikro_orm_migrations
